{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794571821,"nonce":"dw4ZgcBU8t2IJ4bmI4Avkpu9tuU=","message":"HashSet\u003cT\u003e and BTreeSet\u003cT\u003e now cross as typed hxn.Set\u003cT\u003e values, including in records, nested collections, futures, signals, and lent callbacks. Rust inserts decoded elements into the declared collection, applying its normal Eq + Hash or Ord semantics. Returned BTreeSet elements follow Rust's Ord order; HashSet has no promised order. A set of u8 is hxn.Set\u003cInt\u003e, rather than Bytes. Distinct set_ and sorted_set_ take names allow futures of both collections with the same element type.\n\nThe Haxe wrapper offers add, has, remove, clear, length, iteration, copy and toArray. Array literals convert to sets. Construction copies and deduplicates the input; toArray returns a copy. Membership uses Haxe == by default (including object identity), with an optional equality function for data values; Rust still applies its own equality on every call. Array storage preserves insertion or received order, uses linear membership checks, and does not sort later additions. The CLI embeds the wrapper in generated bindings and packages. Runtime checks cover duplicate insertion, absent removal, iteration, copy isolation, custom equality and its preservation by copy, array conversion, and null-set rejection.\n\nSets reuse the list encoding and the bounded collection reader. No runtime ABI implementation, layout, exported symbol, or dependency changed, and hxn-future did not change. The generator previously wrote no bytes for an empty data record, contradicting the reader's one-byte minimum used to bound collection counts. Both generated codecs now use and validate one zero byte for an empty record. This fixes empty-record lists as well as sets and preserves the count bound; adapters and Haxe bindings must be regenerated together for this encoding change.\n\nGenerator checks cover typed fields and nested sets, byte sets, distinct future take exports, manifest names, rejection of interface elements, and empty-record markers. The runtime fixture checks deduplication, sorted output, empty sets, enum elements, numeric range rejection, futures, signals, UTF-8 callback values in both directions, callback failure cleanup, and two empty records remaining two list elements but becoming one set element. The pantry example constructs and adds to an hxn.Set\u003cString\u003e for its HashSet\u003cString\u003e input and receives a sorted set from BTreeSet\u003cString\u003e; its golden output covers duplicate insertion and deterministic order. Both manifests, docs/design.md, docs/conventions.md, and the primary wire-format documentation are current.\n\nValidation, with runtime tests under timeouts:\n- macOS arm64: cargo test --workspace, cargo fmt --all -- --check, and cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings pass. All nine counter modes pass. CLI and examples pass on Ash hybrid/JIT/interpreter, hxcpp, CPPIA, Ash wasm, and Emscripten; C imports pass on all supported modes, including SQLite where supported. Native Ash fixtures were repeated three times per mode, generated hxcpp and CPPIA three times, and generated wasm/Emscripten twice after the initial passes.\n- Linux x86-64 over ssh nuc: workspace tests and strict Clippy pass. Both counter fixtures pass on stock HashLink, and the generated fixture passes on Ash, hxcpp, and CPPIA. CLI, examples, and C imports pass on stock HashLink, hxcpp, CPPIA, and Ash hybrid/JIT/interpreter. The generated program passes three repetitions each on stock HashLink and Ash hybrid/JIT/interpreter. The isolated source copy was verified against SHA-256 hashes of all tracked local files; only temporary task-local Haxe/hxcpp configuration was used.\n- Mobile: the full example suite passes twice each on the iPhone 15 iOS 17.5 simulator using iphonesim and the Pixel 9 Pro Android emulator through ADB. Both devices were shut down after testing.\n- Miri: ABI tests pass (35 tests). Future tests pass (30 tests), with the same four wall-clock-sensitive exclusions and ignore-leaks flag as CI. An initial 240-second future run timed out without a failure; the 900-second retry passed in 470 seconds.\n\nNot run for these uncommitted changes: Windows (awaiting a push and CI), Linux wasm/Emscripten (both paths ran on macOS), or a physical mobile device. The optional Linux SQLite import was skipped because sqlite3.h is absent; macOS native SQLite imports passed. The previously pushed enum-map commit f6ab919 has now passed CI, including Windows Rust, HashLink, and hxcpp.\n\nThe set change remains uncommitted. This broader generator issue stays open for its remaining gaps.","files":null}]}